 This install applies to "Wing Commander II: Vengeance of the Kilrathi", an
 unofficial Amiga port of the 1991 PC game by Origin Systems.

 THIS IS AN EARLY ALPHA. The game is translated from the PC original and
 runs, but it is not optimized yet and has no digitised speech. Please
 read "What to expect" before installing.


 What to expect:
 ---------------
 What has been checked: the translated game code was run in a test build
 and compared step by step against the PC original running under DOSBox-X,
 along these routes: the start and the title, a new game with the intro,
 the pilot's file, the first flight, a mission flown to its end (with the
 game's debug keys and autopilot) and landed, saving and loading a game in
 the quarters, and both ways out to DOS (Alt+X on the title menu, the
 window in the quarters). At every step the screen, the memory and every
 call the game made matched the original. The Amiga build itself was
 compared the same way on an emulated A1200 with a 68030 at the title,
 the intro and the first flight frame.

 What to expect when playing:
 - Sound: the AdLib music and sound effects play on Paula, resynthesised
   from the FM chip the game drives. Their sound has not been measured
   against the PC yet, and some instruments may not sound as on the PC.
   The Sound Blaster's digitised speech is silent in this version.
 - Speed: nothing has been optimized yet. In the one scene measured on an
   emulated 50 MHz 68030, the opening animation, the Amiga draws one frame for
   every eight the PC does. Flight is estimated, not measured, at about
   half the PC's frame rate on that 68030 and close to it on a 40 MHz
   68040. Expect it to be slow. Making it faster comes after the speech.
 - Unsupported paths: the port stops rather than guessing when the game
   does something it does not support yet. The tests cover the routes
   above, not every mission, cinematic or menu choice, nor the ending.
   Under WHDLoad a stop shows the requester "Wing Commander II stopped";
   started from a Shell, a line beginning with WC2_STOP appears. Please
   report it with that line and what you were doing (see Contact).
 - Saving: saving and loading matched the original in the test build, but
   have not been tested under WHDLoad yet. Keep copies of saved games you
   care about, and see the NoWriteCache note under Requirements.
 - Machines: tested only on emulated A1200s (68030, 68040 and 68060, with
   and without WHDLoad's MMU option, PAL and NTSC), not yet on real
   hardware. The start from a Shell without WHDLoad was not tested for
   this release.
 - Input: keyboard and mouse as in the PC original, and a joystick (see
   Playing). The joystick ports have not been tested on real hardware.


 Requirements:
 -------------
 This install requires WHDLoad version 17.0 or better and an AGA Amiga
 (A1200 or A4000) with a 68030 or better; a 68040 or 68060 is recommended.
 The game runs as the PC original's VGA version (256 colours, 320x200).

 It needs 8 MB of Fast memory. The data cache needs the MMU: WHDLoad uses
 it on a 68040 or 68060, on a 68030 only with the WHDLoad option MMU (add
 the tooltype MMU to the icon).

 With PreLoad, WHDLoad (seen with 20.1) can stop at exit on an empty
 Workbench screen after the game has created a file, such as a saved game.
 The new file is then lost. Add the tooltype NoWriteCache to the icon to
 avoid this; WHDLoad then writes files at once.

 The game requires an installed A1200 or A4000 Kickstart 3.1 image.
 The kickstart image must be located in the directory "Devs:Kickstarts" and
 must be named "kick40068.A1200" or "kick40068.A4000". Additionally the file
 "kick40068.A1200.RTB" or "kick40068.A4000.RTB" must be installed in the same
 directory as the kickstart image. It can be found in the archive
 "util/boot/skick346.lha" on aminet.


 Installation:
 -------------
 The install does not include the original game files. It installs them from
 the seven 1.44 MB PC floppies of Wing Commander II. Other releases are not
 supported. Run the Install icon and choose either

 - a drawer containing the disk images

     Disk1.img  Disk2.img  Disk3.img  Disk4.img  Disk5.img  Disk6.img  Disk7.img

   or the files of all seven disks copied into one drawer, or
 - the PC floppies themselves, one after the other in PC0: (CrossDOS, a
   high-density drive).

 Every file is checked; damaged or other disks are refused. The install
 unpacks the files as the original installer does. The installed game takes
 17 MB; the install needs 32 MB free while it runs, 42 MB from PC floppies.

 The game executable "Wc2" is not part of the install archive on the
 WHDLoad WWW-Page. Download it from the matching release at

 https://github.com/Vesuri/wc2/releases

 and the installer will ask for it. The full release archive there already
 includes it.

 To update, select the same parent drawer and choose Keep. Use existing (the
 default) keeps the installed game files; Replace installs them again from the
 disks. Saved games are kept either way.


 Playing:
 --------
 Keyboard and mouse work as in the PC original; keys are at their US
 keyboard positions. See the original manual for the controls. Esc skips
 the cinematics. A joystick in port 2 works as the cursor keys; its two
 buttons work as the left and right mouse buttons. Set the Custom1 tooltype
 to 1 for a proportional (analog) joystick in port 2 instead, and calibrate
 it with Ctrl+J in flight, as in the original. Quit with Alt+X on the
 title menu, or click the window in your quarters (Exit to DOS). The
 WHDLoad QuitKey (Help) quits immediately.


